   "title": null,
   "text": "# Flaky tests and agents\n\n## 1. Recognize the flaky-test loop\n\nA flaky test fails sometimes without any code change. That definition matters when an agent sees a red test: the agent tries to fix the code, even when the test itself is flaky.\n\nThe red result starts the same response, but it does not establish that the code changed. Keep those two points separate. A test can fail, and an agent can try to repair the code, while the test is failing intermittently without a code change.\n\nAgents tend to respond to this situation by rerunning until green or weakening the assertion. Neither response measures how often the test fails on the same commit. Rerunning until green makes the green result the stopping point; measuring flakiness requires counting failures across a fixed set of runs.\n\nThe problem also reaches beyond an individual test. A required gate that sometimes fails teaches agents and people to ignore it. The lesson is to recognize intermittent failure before accepting another green rerun as the end of the work.\n\n## 2. Measure failures and quarantine with ownership\n\nTo measure flakiness, rerun each test 20 times on the same commit and count its failures. Keep the commit the same throughout those runs: the measurement is about failures without a code change.\n\nUse the audit script:\n\n```sh\nscripts/flake-scan.mjs --runs 20 --json\n```\n\nThe count of failures is the measurement. A green result somewhere among the 20 runs does not replace that count. Follow the fixed run count instead of stopping when a rerun turns green.\n\nFor a known-flaky test, quarantine means moving it out of the required gate while it is fixed, with an owner and a date. Include all parts of that definition when describing the action: the test is known to be flaky, it leaves the required gate while being fixed, and it has an owner and a date.\n\nQuarantine addresses the known-flaky test’s place in the gate while the fix is pending. Keep that action distinct from rerunning until green or weakening an assertion.\n\nThe rubric used in this lesson is the author’s own. It does not come from a vendor.",
